LeetCode 641 — Design Circular Deque

From Brute Force → Most Optimal

All approaches + code + explanation + dry run
1. Problem Understanding

A Deque (Double-Ended Queue) allows insertion and deletion from both ends.

We need to implement:

insertFront()
insertLast()


deleteFront()
deleteLast()


getFront()
getRear()


isEmpty()
isFull()

For a circular deque, when we reach the end of the array, we wrap around to the beginning.

For example, capacity = 5:

0 1 2 3 4
↑       ↑
front   rear

After index 4, the next index is:

(4 + 1) % 5 = 0

And before index 0:

(0 - 1 + 5) % 5 = 4

Approach 1 — Simple Array / Shifting
Idea

Use a normal array.

For:

insertFront(value)

we shift all existing elements one position to the right.

Example:

Before:


[10, 20, 30, _, _]

Insert 5 at front:

[5, 10, 20, 30, _]

Similarly, deletion from the front requires shifting elements left.

Code
class MyCircularDeque {
        return true;
    }


    public boolean insertLast(int value) {


        if (isFull()) {
            return false;
        }


        deque[size] = value;
        size++;


        return true;
    }


    public boolean deleteFront() {


        if (isEmpty()) {
            return false;
        }


        // Shift elements left
        for (int i = 0; i < size - 1; i++) {
            deque[i] = deque[i + 1];
        }


        size--;


        return true;
    }


    public boolean deleteLast() {


        if (isEmpty()) {
            return false;
        }


        size--;


        return true;
    }


    public int getFront() {


        if (isEmpty()) {
            return -1;
        }


        return deque[0];
    }


    public int getRear() {


        if (isEmpty()) {
            return -1;
        }


        return deque[size - 1];
    }


    public boolean isEmpty() {
        return size == 0;
    }


    public boolean isFull() {
        return size == capacity;
    }
}

Complexity
Operation	Time
insertFront	O(K)
insertLast	O(1)
deleteFront	O(K)
deleteLast	O(1)
getFront	O(1)
getRear	O(1)

Where K is the deque capacity.

Problem

The major issue is shifting.

We want:

insertFront → O(1)
deleteFront → O(1)

So let's improve it.

Approach 2 — Doubly Linked List

A deque naturally fits a doubly linked list.

Each node has:

prev ← node → next

We maintain:

head
tail

Example:

null ← 10 ⇄ 20 ⇄ 30 → null
        ↑           ↑
       head        tail

Then:

Insert Front
5
↓
5 ⇄ 10 ⇄ 20 ⇄ 30
Insert Last
10 ⇄ 20 ⇄ 30 ⇄ 40
Delete Front

Remove head.

Delete Last

Remove tail.

All these operations are O(1).

Code
class MyCircularDeque {
        Node node = new Node(value);


        if (isEmpty()) {
            head = tail = node;
        } else {
            tail.next = node;
            node.prev = tail;
            tail = node;
        }


        size++;


        return true;
    }


    public boolean deleteFront() {


        if (isEmpty()) {
            return false;
        }


        if (size == 1) {
            head = tail = null;
        } else {
            head = head.next;
            head.prev = null;
        }


        size--;


        return true;
    }


    public boolean deleteLast() {


        if (isEmpty()) {
            return false;
        }


        if (size == 1) {
            head = tail = null;
        } else {
            tail = tail.prev;
            tail.next = null;
        }


        size--;


        return true;
    }


    public int getFront() {


        if (isEmpty()) {
            return -1;
        }


        return head.val;
    }


    public int getRear() {


        if (isEmpty()) {
            return -1;
        }


        return tail.val;
    }


    public boolean isEmpty() {
        return size == 0;
    }


    public boolean isFull() {
        return size == capacity;
    }
}

Complexity
insertFront  → O(1)
insertLast   → O(1)
deleteFront  → O(1)
deleteLast   → O(1)
getFront     → O(1)
getRear      → O(1)

But:

Space = O(K)

because we have K nodes plus pointers.

Approach 3 — Circular Array ⭐

This is the intended and most elegant approach for this problem.

Instead of shifting elements, use a fixed-size circular array.

We maintain:

front
rear
size
capacity

The important formulas are:

Move forward
(index + 1) % capacity
Move backward
(index - 1 + capacity) % capacity

The + capacity is important because Java's % can produce a negative result.

For example:

(0 - 1) % 5

gives:

-1

Instead:

(0 - 1 + 5) % 5

gives:

4
Your Code

Your approach is correct in concept, but there is one important Java bug in the constructor.

You wrote:

int front=0;
int rear=k-1;
int size=0;

These create local variables instead of assigning the class fields.

You need:

front = 0;
rear = k - 1;
size = 0;

or:

this.front = 0;
this.rear = k - 1;
this.size = 0;
Corrected Version of Your Code
class MyCircularDeque {


        dequeue[front] = value;


        size++;


        return true;
    }


    public boolean insertLast(int value) {


        if (isFull()) {
            return false;
        }


        dequeue[rear] = value;


        rear = (rear + 1) % capacity;


        size++;


        return true;
    }


    public boolean deleteFront() {


        if (isEmpty()) {
            return false;
        }


        front = (front + 1) % capacity;


        size--;


        return true;
    }


    public boolean deleteLast() {


        if (isEmpty()) {
            return false;
        }


        rear = (rear - 1 + capacity) % capacity;


        size--;


        return true;
    }


    public int getFront() {


        if (isEmpty()) {
            return -1;
        }


        return dequeue[front];
    }


    public int getRear() {


        if (isEmpty()) {
            return -1;
        }


        return dequeue[(rear - 1 + capacity) % capacity];
    }


    public boolean isEmpty() {
        return size == 0;
    }


    public boolean isFull() {
        return size == capacity;
    }
}

Understanding front and rear

This implementation uses a slightly unusual but very useful convention:

front
 ↓
first element

while:

rear
 ↓
position AFTER the last element

So rear does not directly point to the last element.

That's why:

getRear()

uses:

(rear - 1 + capacity) % capacity
