       20   .  .  .  5

Logical order from front:

5 → 10 → 20

This is the beauty of the circular array.

deleteFront()

Current:

front = 4

Delete the element at 4.

Move front:

front = (front + 1) % capacity;

Therefore:

front = 0

Now:

10 → 20
deleteLast()

Remember:

rear

points to the next available position, not the last element.

So first move backward:

rear = (rear - 1 + capacity) % capacity;

Then the new rear position represents the removed last element.

Why size Is Important

Circular arrays often have a problem:

front == rear

could mean either:

empty

or:

full

depending on the implementation.

We avoid this ambiguity by maintaining:

size

Therefore:

isEmpty()

is simply:

return size == 0;

And:

isFull()

is:

return size == capacity;

This makes the implementation much easier to reason about.

